            'body_en' => "The aqueduct is a solid causeway, not an arcade: a mass of masonry with a shallow channel running along its top. The channel floor was laid on a bed of stone and sealed with a mortar of lime and bitumen, the Assyrian version of waterproof concrete, so the water did not seep away into the fill. Where the stream crossed, the builders opened arches through the mass — pointed in profile, built by corbelling the courses inward until they met, since the true voussoir arch was not yet in use.\n\nThe gradient was the hard part. Over fifty kilometres the canal had to fall gently and continuously, through hills and across valleys, fast enough to keep flowing and slow enough not to scour its bed. Sennacherib\'s scribes say the work took fifteen months, and that when the sluice at Khinis was opened the water came down so well that the king rewarded the diggers with coloured garments and gold rings instead of punishing them.\n\nAt the far end the water reached the Khosr above Nineveh and, through it, the city\'s moats, gardens and parks. Some scholars argue that the \"Hanging Gardens of Babylon\" were in fact these gardens of Nineveh, watered by this system — a hypothesis, not a settled fact, but a tempting one.",
